{"name":"Flight jackets","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG/960px-USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG","caption":"USMC flight jacket & Cobra flight helmet MMH","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Flight jackets are waist-length jackets that began as warm outerwear for open-cockpit pilots and are now an everyday fashion staple. Models such as the A-2, G-1, B-3 and MA-1 are the best known.","For appearance, the short cut, ribbed cuffs and waistband create a defined waist and structured shoulder line, a look many men use to read as put-together. Their history and design are documented; no study measures their effect on attractiveness."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People wear them as a layering piece over tees, knits or hoodies. Leather A-2 and G-1 styles, shearling B-3 styles and nylon MA-1 styles are the usual families, chosen for warmth, colour and fit."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Low risk. Fit is the main practical issue, and leather and shearling versions cost more and need care. Authentic military-pattern pieces are sold as vintage or reproductions."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG/960px-USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/USMC_flight_jacket_%26_Cobra_flight_helmet_MMH.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"USMC flight jacket & Cobra flight helmet MMH","license":"CC BY 3.0","creator":"BrokenSphere